ong>⏱️ Daily Study Time</strong></p></div></div></div></div><div class="stElementContainer element-container st-emotion-cache-3pwa5w e1a2occc1" data-testid="stElementContainer" data-stale="false" width="100%" height="auto" overflow="visible"><div class="stMarkdown" data-testid="stMarkdown"><div class="st-emotion-cache-k2h9z6 e1o1x6gp2"><div class="st-emotion-cache-bzjstw e12uj6qz0" data-testid="stMarkdownContainer"><p>35 minutes per day</p></div></div></div></div><div class="stElementContainer element-container st-emotion-cache-1vo6xi6 e1a2occc1" data-testid="stElementContainer" data-stale="false" width="100%" height="auto" overflow="visible"><div class="stMarkdown" data-testid="stMarkdown"><div class="st-emotion-cache-k2h9z6 e1o1x6gp2"><div class="st-emotion-cache-bzjstw e12uj6qz0" data-testid="stMarkdownContainer"><hr></div></div></div></div><div class="stElementContainer element-container st-emotion-cache-3pwa5w e1a2occc1" data-testid="stElementContainer" data-stale="false" width="100%" height="auto" overflow="visible"><div class="stMarkdown" data-testid="stMarkdown"><div class="st-emotion-cache-k2h9z6 e1o1x6gp2"><div class="st-emotion-cache-bzjstw e12uj6qz0" data-testid="stMarkdownContainer"><div data-testid="stHeadingWithActionElements" class="st-emotion-cache-npkxw2 e12uj6qz2"><h3 id="your-personalized-study-schedule" aria-labelledby=":rf:" style="color: rgb(185, 154, 255);"><span data-heading-text="" class="st-emotion-cache-0 e12uj6qz3" id=":rf:">
    🗓️ Your Personalized Study Schedule
</span><span data-testid="stHeaderActionElements" class="st-emotion-cache-1i6bcf7 e12uj6qz4"><a href="http://localhost:8501/#your-personalized-study-schedule" aria-label="Link to heading" class="st-emotion-cache-12na9ak e12uj6qz1"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M15 7h3a5 5 0 0 1 5 5 5 5 0 0 1-5 5h-3m-6 0H6a5 5 0 0 1-5-5 5 5 0 0 1 5-5h3"></path><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="16" y2="12"></line></svg></a></span></h3></div></div></div></div></div><div class="stElementContainer element-container st-emotion-cache-3pwa5w e1a2occc1" data-testid="stElementContainer" data-stale="false" width="100%" height="auto" overflow="visible"><div class="stMarkdown" data-testid="stMarkdown"><div class="st-emotion-cache-k2h9z6 e1o1x6gp2"><div class="st-emotion-cache-bzjstw e12uj6qz0" data-testid="stMarkdownContainer"><p>Hello there! I'm your AI Personalized Learning Coach, and I'm excited to help you master OOPs in Java. This 8-day plan is designed to be practical, focused, and perfectly fit into your 35-minute daily study schedule.</p>
<p>Let's dive in and unlock the power of Object-Oriented Programming!</p>
<hr>
<div data-testid="stHeadingWithActionElements" class="st-emotion-cache-npkxw2 e12uj6qz2"><h2 id="java-oops-study-plan-8-days-to-mastery" aria-labelledby=":rg:"><span data-heading-text="" class="st-emotion-cache-0 e12uj6qz3" id=":rg:"><strong>Java OOPs Study Plan: 8 Days to Mastery</strong></span><span data-testid="stHeaderActionElements" class="st-emotion-cache-1i6bcf7 e12uj6qz4"><a href="http://localhost:8501/#java-oops-study-plan-8-days-to-mastery" aria-label="Link to heading" class="st-emotion-cache-12na9ak e12uj6qz1"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M15 7h3a5 5 0 0 1 5 5 5 5 0 0 1-5 5h-3m-6 0H6a5 5 0 0 1-5-5 5 5 0 0 1 5-5h3"></path><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="16" y2="12"></line></svg></a></span></h2></div>
<p><strong>Student Details:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Subject:</strong> Java</li>
<li><strong>Learning Goal:</strong> OOPs Concepts</li>
<li><strong>Knowledge Level:</strong> Intermediate</li>
<li><strong>Duration:</strong> 8 days</li>
<li><strong>Daily Study Time:</strong> 35 minutes</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<div data-testid="stHeadingWithActionElements" class="st-emotion-cache-npkxw2 e12uj6qz2"><h3 id="important-notes-before-you-begin" aria-labelledby=":rh:"><span data-heading-text="" class="st-emotion-cache-0 e12uj6qz3" id=":rh:"><strong>Important Notes Before You Begin:</strong></span><span data-testid="stHeaderActionElements" class="st-emotion-cache-1i6bcf7 e12uj6qz4"><a href="http://localhost:8501/#important-notes-before-you-begin" aria-label="Link to heading" class="st-emotion-cache-12na9ak e12uj6qz1"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M15 7h3a5 5 0 0 1 5 5 5 5 0 0 1-5 5h-3m-6 0H6a5 5 0 0 1-5-5 5 5 0 0 1 5-5h3"></path><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="16" y2="12"></line></svg></a></span></h3></div>
<ul>
<li><strong>Consistency is Key:</strong> Stick to your 35 minutes each day. Even short, focused sessions are more effective than sporadic long ones.</li>
<li><strong>Hands-on Practice:</strong> The exercises are crucial. Type out the code yourself, don't just read it. Experiment!</li>
<li><strong>Tools:</strong> Have a Java Development Kit (JDK) and an Integrated Development Environment (IDE) like IntelliJ IDEA or Eclipse ready to go. Even a simple text editor and command line will work.</li>
<li><strong>Resources:</strong> Have a good online Java tutorial (e.g., Oracle Java Tutorials, GeeksforGeeks, Baeldung) handy for quick reference if you get stuck.</li>
<li><strong>Questions:</strong> If you encounter difficulties, make a note and revisit them during the revision sessions or search for solutions online.</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<div data-testid="stHeadingWithActionElements" class="st-emotion-cache-npkxw2 e12uj6qz2"><h3 id="day-1-introduction-to-oop-classes-and-objects" aria-labelledby=":ri:"><span data-heading-text="" class="st-emotion-cache-0 e12uj6qz3" id=":ri:"><strong>Day 1: Introduction to OOP, Classes &amp; Objects</strong></span><span data-testid="stHeaderActionElements" class="st-emotion-cache-1i6bcf7 e12uj6qz4"><a href="http://localhost:8501/#day-1-introduction-to-oop-classes-and-objects" aria-label="Link to heading" class="st-emotion-cache-12na9ak e12uj6qz1"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M15 7h3a5 5 0 0 1 5 5 5 5 0 0 1-5 5h-3m-6 0H6a5 5 0 0 1-5-5 5 5 0 0 1 5-5h3"></path><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="16" y2="12"></line></svg></a></span></h3></div>
<ul>
<li><strong>Daily Topic:</strong> Understanding OOP Principles &amp; Creating Your First Class and Object</li>
<li><strong>Learning Objectives:</strong>
<ul>
<li>Understand the fundamental concept of Object-Oriented Programming (OOP).</li>
<li>Differentiate between a Class and an Object.</li>
<li>Learn to declare a basic Java class with attributes (fields) and behaviors (methods).</li>
<li>Learn to create an object (instance) of a class.</li>
<li>Access object attributes and call object methods.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Study Activities (15 minutes):</strong>
<ul>
<li>Read a concise explanation of "What is OOP?" and its four pillars (Encapsulation, Inheritance, Polymorphism, Abstraction) at a high level.</li>
<li>Understand the analogy: Class as a blueprint, Object as a house built from the blueprint.</li>
<li>Study the syntax for defining a simple class and creating an object.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Practice Exercises (15 minutes):</strong>
<ul>
<li><strong>Task 1:</strong> Create a Java class named <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Dog</code>.
<ul>
<li>Give it attributes: <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">String name</code>, <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">String breed</code>, <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">int age</code>.</li>
<li>Give it a method: <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">void bark()</code> that prints "Woof!".</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Task 2:</strong> In your <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">main</code> method:
<ul>
<li>Create two <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Dog</code> objects: <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">myDog</code> and <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">yourDog</code>.</li>
<li>Assign values to their attributes (e.g., <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">myDog.name = "Buddy"</code>).</li>
<li>Call the <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">bark()</code> method for both objects.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Estimated Time:</strong> 30 minutes</li>
<li><strong>Quick Review/Self-Check (5 minutes):</strong>
<ul>
<li>What's the difference between a class and an object?</li>
<li>Can you create an object without a class? Why or why not?</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<div data-testid="stHeadingWithActionElements" class="st-emotion-cache-npkxw2 e12uj6qz2"><h3 id="day-2-constructors-and-the-this-keyword" aria-labelledby=":rj:"><span data-heading-text="" class="st-emotion-cache-0 e12uj6qz3" id=":rj:"><strong>Day 2: Constructors and the <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">this</code> Keyword</strong></span><span data-testid="stHeaderActionElements" class="st-emotion-cache-1i6bcf7 e12uj6qz4"><a href="http://localhost:8501/#day-2-constructors-and-the-this-keyword" aria-label="Link to heading" class="st-emotion-cache-12na9ak e12uj6qz1"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M15 7h3a5 5 0 0 1 5 5 5 5 0 0 1-5 5h-3m-6 0H6a5 5 0 0 1-5-5 5 5 0 0 1 5-5h3"></path><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="16" y2="12"></line></svg></a></span></h3></div>
<ul>
<li><strong>Daily Topic:</strong> Initializing Objects with Constructors</li>
<li><strong>Learning Objectives:</strong>
<ul>
<li>Understand the purpose of a constructor.</li>
<li>Learn to define a default (no-argument) constructor.</li>
<li>Learn to define parameterized constructors to initialize object state upon creation.</li>
<li>Understand and use the <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">this</code> keyword to refer to the current object's instance variables.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Study Activities (15 minutes):</strong>
<ul>
<li>Read about constructors: what they are, when they are called, and the rules for defining them.</li>
<li>Learn about the default constructor provided automatically if you don't define any.</li>
<li>Understand how <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">this</code> keyword helps differentiate between instance variables and local parameters (especially in constructors).</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Practice Exercises (15 minutes):</strong>
<ul>
<li><strong>Task 1:</strong> Modify your <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Dog</code> class from Day 1.
<ul>
<li>Add a default constructor that prints "A new dog is born!".</li>
<li>Add a parameterized constructor: <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">public Dog(String name, String breed, int age)</code> that initializes all attributes.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Task 2:</strong> In your <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">main</code> method:
<ul>
<li>Create a <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Dog</code> object using the default constructor.</li>
<li>Create another <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Dog</code> object using the parameterized constructor (e.g., <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Dog newDog = new Dog("Max", "Golden Retriever", 3);</code>).</li>
<li>Print the details of the <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">newDog</code> to confirm initialization.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Estimated Time:</strong> 30 minutes</li>
<li><strong>Quick Review/Self-Check (5 minutes):</strong>
<ul>
<li>When is a constructor called?</li>
<li>Why is <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">this</code> keyword useful in a constructor?</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<div data-testid="stHeadingWithActionElements" class="st-emotion-cache-npkxw2 e12uj6qz2"><h3 id="day-3-encapsulation-and-access-modifiers" aria-labelledby=":rk:"><span data-heading-text="" class="st-emotion-cache-0 e12uj6qz3" id=":rk:"><strong>Day 3: Encapsulation and Access Modifiers</strong></span><span data-testid="stHeaderActionElements" class="st-emotion-cache-1i6bcf7 e12uj6qz4"><a href="http://localhost:8501/#day-3-encapsulation-and-access-modifiers" aria-label="Link to heading" class="st-emotion-cache-12na9ak e12uj6qz1"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M15 7h3a5 5 0 0 1 5 5 5 5 0 0 1-5 5h-3m-6 0H6a5 5 0 0 1-5-5 5 5 0 0 1 5-5h3"></path><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="16" y2="12"></line></svg></a></span></h3></div>
<ul>
<li><strong>Daily Topic:</strong> Protecting Your Data with Encapsulation</li>
<li><strong>Learning Objectives:</strong>
<ul>
<li>Understand the concept of Encapsulation (data hiding).</li>
<li>Learn to use <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">private</code> access modifier for instance variables.</li>
<li>Learn to create <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">public</code> getter (accessor) and setter (mutator) methods.</li>
<li>Understand the benefits of encapsulation (flexibility, control over data).</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Study Activities (15 minutes):</strong>
<ul>
<li>Read about Encapsulation as one of the four OOP pillars.</li>
<li>Focus on <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">private</code> and <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">public</code> access modifiers.</li>
<li>Understand the standard naming conventions for getters (<code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">getVariableName()</code>) and setters (<code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">setVariableName(newValue)</code>).</li>
<li>Briefly understand the other access modifiers: <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">protected</code> and <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">default</code> (package-private).</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Practice Exercises (15 minutes):</strong>
<ul>
<li><strong>Task 1:</strong> Modify your <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Dog</code> class.
<ul>
<li>Change all instance variables (<code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">name</code>, <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">breed</code>, <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">age</code>) to <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">private</code>.</li>
<li>Generate or manually write <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">public</code> getter methods for <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">name</code>, <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">breed</code>, and <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">age</code>.</li>
<li>Generate or manually write <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">public</code> setter methods for <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">name</code>, <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">breed</code>, and <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">age</code>.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Task 2:</strong> In your <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">main</code> method:
<ul>
<li>Try to directly access <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">myDog.name = "Sparky";</code> (it should cause a compile-time error).</li>
<li>Use the setter method: <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">myDog.setName("Sparky");</code>.</li>
<li>Use the getter methods to retrieve and print <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">myDog.getName()</code>, <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">myDog.getBreed()</code>, <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">myDog.getAge()</code>.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Estimated Time:</strong> 30 minutes</li>
<li><strong>Quick Review/Self-Check (5 minutes):</strong>
<ul>
<li>What problem does <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">private</code> solve?</li>
<li>Why are getters and setters typically <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">public</code>?</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<div data-testid="stHeadingWithActionElements" class="st-emotion-cache-npkxw2 e12uj6qz2"><h3 id="day-4-inheritance-basics-and-extends-keyword" aria-labelledby=":rl:"><span data-heading-text="" class="st-emotion-cache-0 e12uj6qz3" id=":rl:"><strong>Day 4: Inheritance - Basics &amp; <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">extends</code> Keyword</strong></span><span data-testid="stHeaderActionElements" class="st-emotion-cache-1i6bcf7 e12uj6qz4"><a href="http://localhost:8501/#day-4-inheritance-basics-and-extends-keyword" aria-label="Link to heading" class="st-emotion-cache-12na9ak e12uj6qz1"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M15 7h3a5 5 0 0 1 5 5 5 5 0 0 1-5 5h-3m-6 0H6a5 5 0 0 1-5-5 5 5 0 0 1 5-5h3"></path><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="16" y2="12"></line></svg></a></span></h3></div>
<ul>
<li><strong>Daily Topic:</strong> Reusing Code with Inheritance</li>
<li><strong>Learning Objectives:</strong>
<ul>
<li>Understand the concept of Inheritance (is-a relationship).</li>
<li>Learn to create a superclass (parent class) and a subclass (child class).</li>
<li>Use the <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">extends</code> keyword to establish an inheritance relationship.</li>
<li>Understand how subclasses inherit attributes and methods from their superclass.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Study Activities (15 minutes):</strong>
<ul>
<li>Read about Inheritance: how it promotes code reusability and forms an "is-a" relationship (e.g., a Car <em>is a</em> Vehicle).</li>
<li>Understand the <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">extends</code> keyword syntax.</li>
<li>Learn what members are inherited and what aren't (private members are not directly accessible but exist).</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Practice Exercises (15 minutes):</strong>
<ul>
<li><strong>Task 1:</strong> Create a new class <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Animal</code>.
<ul>
<li>Attributes: <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">private String species</code>, <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">private int numberOfLegs</code>.</li>
<li>Methods: <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">public void eat() { System.out.println("Animal is eating."); }</code>, <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">public String getSpecies()</code>, <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">public void setSpecies(String species)</code>.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Task 2:</strong> Modify your <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Dog</code> class to extend <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Animal</code>.
<ul>
<li>Remove <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">breed</code> and <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">age</code> for now (we'll add them back smart). <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Dog extends Animal</code>.</li>
<li>The <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Dog</code> class should now implicitly have <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">species</code>, <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">numberOfLegs</code>, and the <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">eat()</code> method.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Task 3:</strong> In your <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">main</code> method:
<ul>
<li>Create a <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Dog</code> object.</li>
<li>Set its <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">species</code> (using <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">setSpecies()</code>) and call its <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">eat()</code> method.</li>
<li>Observe that <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Dog</code> can use <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Animal</code>'s methods.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Estimated Time:</strong> 30 minutes</li>
<li><strong>Quick Review/Self-Check (5 minutes):</strong>
<ul>
<li>What does the <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">extends</code> keyword do?</li>
<li>If <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Dog extends Animal</code>, can <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Dog</code> objects access <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Animal</code>'s <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">private</code> variables directly?</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<div data-testid="stHeadingWithActionElements" class="st-emotion-cache-npkxw2 e12uj6qz2"><h3 id="day-5-inheritance-super-keyword-and-method-overriding" aria-labelledby=":rm:"><span data-heading-text="" class="st-emotion-cache-0 e12uj6qz3" id=":rm:"><strong>Day 5: Inheritance - <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">super</code> Keyword &amp; Method Overriding</strong></span><span data-testid="stHeaderActionElements" class="st-emotion-cache-1i6bcf7 e12uj6qz4"><a href="http://localhost:8501/#day-5-inheritance-super-keyword-and-method-overriding" aria-label="Link to heading" class="st-emotion-cache-12na9ak e12uj6qz1"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M15 7h3a5 5 0 0 1 5 5 5 5 0 0 1-5 5h-3m-6 0H6a5 5 0 0 1-5-5 5 5 0 0 1 5-5h3"></path><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="16" y2="12"></line></svg></a></span></h3></div>
<ul>
<li><strong>Daily Topic:</strong> Enhancing Inherited Behavior</li>
<li><strong>Learning Objectives:</strong>
<ul>
<li>Understand and use the <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">super</code> keyword to call the superclass's constructor.</li>
<li>Understand and use the <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">super</code> keyword to call a superclass's method.</li>
<li>Learn about Method Overriding: providing a specific implementation of a method that is already defined in the superclass.</li>
<li>Use the <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">@Override</code> annotation for clarity and error checking.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Study Activities (15 minutes):</strong>
<ul>
<li>Read about <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">super()</code> to call the parent constructor (must be the first statement in the subclass constructor).</li>
<li>Read about <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">super.methodName()</code> to call the parent method.</li>
<li>Focus on Method Overriding rules: same method signature (name, parameters, return type).</li>
<li>Understand the importance of the <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">@Override</code> annotation.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Practice Exercises (15 minutes):</strong>
<ul>
<li><strong>Task 1:</strong> Enhance <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Animal</code> and <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Dog</code> classes.
<ul>
<li>In <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Animal</code>, add a constructor: <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">public Animal(String species, int numberOfLegs)</code>.</li>
<li>In <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Animal</code>, add a <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">public void makeSound() { System.out.println("Animal makes a sound."); }</code>.</li>
<li>In <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Dog</code>, add back <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">private String breed</code> and <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">private int age</code>.</li>
<li>In <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Dog</code>, create a constructor <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">public Dog(String species, int numberOfLegs, String breed, int age)</code>.
<ul>
<li><strong>Crucially:</strong> Use <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">super(species, numberOfLegs);</code> as the first line.</li>
<li>Initialize <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">this.breed</code> and <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">this.age</code>.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Task 2:</strong> Override <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">makeSound()</code> in <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Dog</code>.
<ul>
<li>Add <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">@Override</code> annotation: <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">public void makeSound() { System.out.println("Woof woof!"); }</code>.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Task 3:</strong> In your <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">main</code> method:
<ul>
<li>Create a <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Dog</code> object using the new constructor.</li>
<li>Call <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">myDog.makeSound()</code>. What does it print?</li>
<li>(Optional challenge) Call <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">super.makeSound()</code> inside <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Dog</code>'s overridden method to show both messages.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Estimated Time:</strong> 30 minutes</li>
<li><strong>Quick Review/Self-Check (5 minutes):</strong>
<ul>
<li>When do you use <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">super()</code> in a constructor?</li>
<li>What's the difference between Method Overloading (different parameters) and Method Overriding (same signature in subclass)?</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<div data-testid="stHeadingWithActionElements" class="st-emotion-cache-npkxw2 e12uj6qz2"><h3 id="day-6-polymorphism" aria-labelledby=":rn:"><span data-heading-text="" class="st-emotion-cache-0 e12uj6qz3" id=":rn:"><strong>Day 6: Polymorphism</strong></span><span data-testid="stHeaderActionElements" class="st-emotion-cache-1i6bcf7 e12uj6qz4"><a href="http://localhost:8501/#day-6-polymorphism" aria-label="Link to heading" class="st-emotion-cache-12na9ak e12uj6qz1"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M15 7h3a5 5 0 0 1 5 5 5 5 0 0 1-5 5h-3m-6 0H6a5 5 0 0 1-5-5 5 5 0 0 1 5-5h3"></path><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="16" y2="12"></line></svg></a></span></h3></div>
<ul>
<li><strong>Daily Topic:</strong> Objects Taking Many Forms</li>
<li><strong>Learning Objectives:</strong>
<ul>
<li>Understand the concept of Polymorphism (many forms).</li>
<li>Learn about runtime polymorphism (dynamic method dispatch).</li>
<li>Understand how an object of a subclass can be referred to by a superclass reference type (upcasting).</li>
<li>See how overridden methods are called based on the <em>actual</em> object type at runtime.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Study Activities (15 minutes):</strong>
<ul>
<li>Read about Polymorphism, emphasizing that it allows you to treat objects of different classes in a unified way if they share a common superclass or interface.</li>
<li>Focus on upcasting (<code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Animal myDog = new Dog(...)</code>).</li>
<li>Understand that when an overridden method is called through a superclass reference, the version in the <em>actual</em> object's class is executed.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Practice Exercises (15 minutes):</strong>
<ul>
<li><strong>Task 1:</strong> Create another class that extends <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Animal</code>, e.g., <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Cat</code>.
<ul>
<li>Give <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Cat</code> a constructor <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">public Cat(String species, int numberOfLegs, String favoriteToy)</code>.</li>
<li>Override the <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">makeSound()</code> method in <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Cat</code> to print "Meow!".</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Task 2:</strong> In your <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">main</code> method:
<ul>
<li>Create an array or <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">ArrayList</code> of <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Animal</code> objects: <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Animal[] animals = new Animal[3];</code>.</li>
<li>Add a <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Dog</code> object, a <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Cat</code> object, and an <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Animal</code> object (if <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Animal</code> is not abstract) to this array.</li>
<li>Loop through the array and call <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">animal.makeSound()</code> for each object. Observe that the correct <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">makeSound()</code> (Dog's, Cat's, or Animal's) is called.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Estimated Time:</strong> 30 minutes</li>
<li><strong>Quick Review/Self-Check (5 minutes):</strong>
<ul>
<li>What is "dynamic method dispatch" in your own words?</li>
<li>If you have <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Animal a = new Dog();</code>, and both <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Animal</code> and <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Dog</code> have a <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">play()</code> method (with <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Dog</code> overriding it), which <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">play()</code> method is called when you execute <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">a.play()</code>?</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<div data-testid="stHeadingWithActionElements" class="st-emotion-cache-npkxw2 e12uj6qz2"><h3 id="day-7-abstraction-abstract-classes-and-methods" aria-labelledby=":ro:"><span data-heading-text="" class="st-emotion-cache-0 e12uj6qz3" id=":ro:"><strong>Day 7: Abstraction - Abstract Classes &amp; Methods</strong></span><span data-testid="stHeaderActionElements" class="st-emotion-cache-1i6bcf7 e12uj6qz4"><a href="http://localhost:8501/#day-7-abstraction-abstract-classes-and-methods" aria-label="Link to heading" class="st-emotion-cache-12na9ak e12uj6qz1"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M15 7h3a5 5 0 0 1 5 5 5 5 0 0 1-5 5h-3m-6 0H6a5 5 0 0 1-5-5 5 5 0 0 1 5-5h3"></path><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="16" y2="12"></line></svg></a></span></h3></div>
<ul>
<li><strong>Daily Topic:</strong> Hiding Complex Details with Abstraction</li>
<li><strong>Learning Objectives:</strong>
<ul>
<li>Understand the concept of Abstraction (showing essential details, hiding implementation).</li>
<li>Learn to declare an <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">abstract</code> class and an <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">abstract</code> method.</li>
<li>Understand the rules for abstract classes (cannot be instantiated, must be extended).</li>
<li>Understand that abstract methods must be implemented by concrete subclasses.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Study Activities (15 minutes):</strong>
<ul>
<li>Read about Abstraction, contrasting it with Encapsulation (Abstraction is about <em>what</em> to do, Encapsulation is about <em>how</em>).</li>
<li>Understand the <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">abstract</code> keyword for classes and methods.</li>
<li>Learn the rules: an abstract class can have concrete and abstract methods. A concrete class extending an abstract class <em>must</em> implement all abstract methods.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Practice Exercises (15 minutes):</strong>
<ul>
<li><strong>Task 1:</strong> Modify your <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Animal</code> class.
<ul>
<li>Make <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Animal</code> an <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">abstract</code> class: <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">public abstract class Animal { ... }</code>.</li>
<li>Make <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">makeSound()</code> an <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">abstract</code> method: <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">public abstract void makeSound();</code>. (Remove its body).</li>
<li>(Optional) Add another abstract method: <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">public abstract void move();</code>.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Task 2:</strong> Your <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Dog</code> and <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Cat</code> classes should now have a compile error if they don't implement <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">makeSound()</code>. Ensure they do (which they already should from Day 5/6).
<ul>
<li>If you added <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">move()</code>, implement <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">move()</code> in both <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Dog</code> ("Dog runs.") and <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Cat</code> ("Cat pounces.").</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Task 3:</strong> In your <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">main</code> method:
<ul>
<li>Try to create <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Animal genericAnimal = new Animal(...);</code> (it should cause a compile-time error).</li>
<li>Continue to create <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Dog</code> and <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Cat</code> objects, and call their <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">makeSound()</code> and <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">move()</code> methods.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Estimated Time:</strong> 30 minutes</li>
<li><strong>Quick Review/Self-Check (5 minutes):</strong>
<ul>
<li>What is the main purpose of an abstract class?</li>
<li>Can an abstract class have a constructor? If so, when is it called?</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<div data-testid="stHeadingWithActionElements" class="st-emotion-cache-npkxw2 e12uj6qz2"><h3 id="day-8-abstraction-interfaces-and-final-review" aria-labelledby=":rp:"><span data-heading-text="" class="st-emotion-cache-0 e12uj6qz3" id=":rp:"><strong>Day 8: Abstraction - Interfaces &amp; Final Review</strong></span><span data-testid="stHeaderActionElements" class="st-emotion-cache-1i6bcf7 e12uj6qz4"><a href="http://localhost:8501/#day-8-abstraction-interfaces-and-final-review" aria-label="Link to heading" class="st-emotion-cache-12na9ak e12uj6qz1"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M15 7h3a5 5 0 0 1 5 5 5 5 0 0 1-5 5h-3m-6 0H6a5 5 0 0 1-5-5 5 5 0 0 1 5-5h3"></path><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="16" y2="12"></line></svg></a></span></h3></div>
<ul>
<li><strong>Daily Topic:</strong> Achieving Full Abstraction with Interfaces</li>
<li><strong>Learning Objectives:</strong>
<ul>
<li>Understand the concept of an Interface and its purpose.</li>
<li>Learn to declare an <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">interface</code> using the <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">interface</code> keyword.</li>
<li>Use the <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">implements</code> keyword to make a class adhere to an interface contract.</li>
<li>Understand the key differences between abstract classes and interfaces.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Study Activities (15 minutes):</strong>
<ul>
<li>Read about Interfaces: a contract that classes can agree to follow. All methods are implicitly <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">public abstract</code> (before Java 8).</li>
<li>Understand the <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">implements</code> keyword. A class can implement multiple interfaces.</li>
<li>Compare and contrast Abstract Classes vs. Interfaces (e.g., single inheritance vs. multiple inheritance of type). Briefly mention default and static methods in Java 8+ interfaces.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Practice Exercises (15 minutes):</strong>
<ul>
<li><strong>Task 1:</strong> Create an interface named <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Pet</code>.
<ul>
<li>Give it two methods: <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">public void play();</code> and <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">public void beFriendly();</code>.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Task 2:</strong> Modify your <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Dog</code> and <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Cat</code> classes to implement the <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Pet</code> interface.
<ul>
<li><code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">public class Dog extends Animal implements Pet { ... }</code></li>
<li>Implement the <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">play()</code> and <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">beFriendly()</code> methods in both <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Dog</code> and <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Cat</code> with specific behaviors (e.g., "Dog fetches.", "Cat purrs.").</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Task 3:</strong> In your <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">main</code> method:
<ul>
<li>Create an array or <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">ArrayList</code> of <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Pet</code> objects.</li>
<li>Add your <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Dog</code> and <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Cat</code> objects to this array.</li>
<li>Loop through the array and call <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">pet.play()</code> and <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">pet.beFriendly()</code> for each.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Estimated Time:</strong> 30 minutes</li>
<li><strong>Quick Review/Self-Check (5 minutes):</strong>
<ul>
<li>What is the primary difference in how a class interacts with an abstract class versus an interface?</li>
<li>When would you choose an interface over an abstract class?</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
<hr>
<div data-testid="stHeadingWithActionElements" class="st-emotion-cache-npkxw2 e12uj6qz2"><h3 id="final-assessment-self-evaluation-and-coding-challenge" aria-labelledby=":rq:"><span data-heading-text="" class="st-emotion-cache-0 e12uj6qz3" id=":rq:"><strong>Final Assessment (Self-Evaluation &amp; Coding Challenge)</strong></span><span data-testid="stHeaderActionElements" class="st-emotion-cache-1i6bcf7 e12uj6qz4"><a href="http://localhost:8501/#final-assessment-self-evaluation-and-coding-challenge" aria-label="Link to heading" class="st-emotion-cache-12na9ak e12uj6qz1"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M15 7h3a5 5 0 0 1 5 5 5 5 0 0 1-5 5h-3m-6 0H6a5 5 0 0 1-5-5 5 5 0 0 1 5-5h3"></path><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="16" y2="12"></line></svg></a></span></h3></div>
<p>This assessment should take about <strong>30-40 minutes</strong>, so consider doing it on a separate day or dedicating a longer session.</p>
<p><strong>Part 1: Conceptual Questions (10-15 minutes)</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>Explain the four pillars of OOP (Encapsulation, Inheritance, Polymorphism, Abstraction) in your own words, providing a simple example for each.</li>
<li>What is the purpose of a constructor? Can a class have multiple constructors?</li>
<li>Describe the <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">this</code> and <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">super</code> keywords. When would you use each?</li>
<li>What are the key differences between an abstract class and an interface?</li>
<li>When does Method Overloading occur, and when does Method Overriding occur?</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Part 2: Coding Challenge (20-25 minutes)</strong></p>
<p>Design a simple system for a "Shape Hierarchy".</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Abstraction (Abstract Class):</strong>
<ul>
<li>Create an <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">abstract</code> class <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Shape</code>.</li>
<li>It should have a <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">private</code> attribute <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">String color</code>.</li>
<li>It should have a parameterized constructor <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">public Shape(String color)</code>.</li>
<li>It should have a <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">public String getColor()</code> method.</li>
<li>It should have two <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">abstract</code> methods: <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">public abstract double getArea();</code> and <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">public abstract void draw();</code>.</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Inheritance &amp; Encapsulation:</strong>
<ul>
<li>Create two concrete classes: <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Circle</code> and <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Rectangle</code>, both extending <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Shape</code>.</li>
<li><code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Circle</code> should have a <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">private double radius</code>.</li>
<li><code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Rectangle</code> should have <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">private double length</code> and <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">private double width</code>.</li>
<li>Implement appropriate parameterized constructors for both <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Circle</code> and <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Rectangle</code> that call <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">super()</code> and initialize their unique attributes.</li>
<li>Implement <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">getArea()</code> and <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">draw()</code> methods in both <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Circle</code> and <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Rectangle</code>.
<ul>
<li>For <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Circle.getArea()</code>: <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Math.PI * radius * radius</code>.</li>
<li>For <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Rectangle.getArea()</code>: <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">length * width</code>.</li>
<li>For <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">draw()</code> methods, print a descriptive string (e.g., "Drawing a red Circle with radius 5.").</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><strong>Polymorphism:</strong>
<ul>
<li>In a <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">main</code> method, create an <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">ArrayList</code> of <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Shape</code> objects.</li>
<li>Add at least one <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Circle</code> and one <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">Rectangle</code> to the list.</li>
<li>Loop through the list, call <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">draw()</code> and print the <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">getArea()</code> for each shape.</li>
<li>Observe how the correct <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">getArea()</code> and <code class="st-emotion-cache-155dgk4 ej556a80">draw()</code> methods are invoked based on the actual object type.</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ol>
<hr>
<div data-testid="stHeadingWithActionElements" class="st-emotion-cache-npkxw2 e12uj6qz2"><h3 id="tips-for-continued-success" aria-labelledby=":rr:"><span data-heading-text="" class="st-emotion-cache-0 e12uj6qz3" id=":rr:"><strong>Tips for Continued Success:</strong></span><span data-testid="stHeaderActionElements" class="st-emotion-cache-1i6bcf7 e12uj6qz4"><a href="http://localhost:8501/#tips-for-continued-success" aria-label="Link to heading" class="st-emotion-cache-12na9ak e12uj6qz1"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M15 7h3a5 5 0 0 1 5 5 5 5 0 0 1-5 5h-3m-6 0H6a5 5 0 0 1-5-5 5 5 0 0 1 5-5h3"></path><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="16" y2="12"></line></svg></a></span></h3></div>
<ul>
<li><strong>Practice Regularly:</strong> OOPs concepts solidify with repeated application.</li>
<li><strong>Read Code:</strong> Look at open-source Java projects or well-structured examples online to see how OOP is applied in real-world scenarios.</li>
<li><strong>Think OOP:</strong> Before writing new code, think about how you can model it using classes, objects, and relationships.</li>
<li><strong>Build Small Projects:</strong> After this plan, try building a slightly larger project (e.g., a simple library management system, a basic game) applying all these OOP principles.</li>
</ul>
<p>You've got this! By consistently following this plan, you'll build a strong foundation in Java OOPs, which is essential for becoming a proficient Java developer. Good luck!</p></div></div></div></div><div class="stElementContainer element-container st-emotion-cache-1vo6xi6 e1a2occc1" data-testid="stElementContainer" data-stale="false" width="100%" height="auto" overflow="visible"><div class="stMarkdown" data-testid="stMarkdown"><div class="st-emotion-cache-k2h9z6 e1o1x6gp2"><div class="st-emotion-cache-bzjstw e12uj6qz0" data-testid="stMarkdownContainer"><hr></div></div></div></div><div class="stElementContainer element-container st-emotion-cache-3pwa5w e1a2occc1" data-testid="stElementContainer" data-stale="false" width="100%" height="auto" overflow="visible"><div class="stHeading" data-testid="stHeading"><div data-testid="stMarkdownContainer" class="st-emotion-cache-bzjstw e12uj6qz0"><div data-testid="stHeadingWithActionElements" class="st-emotion-cache-npkxw2 e12uj6qz2"><h3 id="adaptive-learning-guidance" aria-labelledby=":rs:"><span data-heading-text="" class="st-emotion-cache-0 e12uj6qz3" id=":rs:">🧠 Adaptive Learning Guidance</span><span data-testid="stHeaderActionElements" class="st-emotion-cache-1i6bcf7 e12uj6qz4"><a href="http://localhost:8501/#adaptive-learning-guidance" aria-label="Link to heading" class="st-emotion-cache-12na9ak e12uj6qz1"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M15 7h3a5 5 0 0 1 5 5 5 5 0 0 1-5 5h-3m-6 0H6a5 5 0 0 1-5-5 5 5 0 0 1 5-5h3"></path><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="16" y2="12"></line></svg></a></span></h3></div></div></div></div><div class="stElementContainer element-container st-emotion-cache-3pwa5w e1a2occc1" data-testid="stElementContainer" data-stale="false" width="100%" height="auto" overflow="visible"><div class="stMarkdown" data-testid="stMarkdown"><div class="st-emotion-cache-k2h9z6 e1o1x6gp2"><div class="st-emotion-cache-bzjstw e12uj6qz0" data-testid="stMarkdownContainer"><p><strong>Topic to focus on:</strong> oops</p></div></div></div></div><div class="stElementContainer element-container st-emotion-cache-3pwa5w e1a2occc1" data-testid="stElementContainer" data-stale="false" width="100%" height="auto" overflow="visible"><div class="stMarkdown" data-testid="stMarkdown"><div class="st-emotion-cache-k2h9z6 e1o1x6gp2"><div class="st-emotion-cache-bzjstw e12uj6qz0" data-testid="stMarkdownContainer"><p><strong>Latest Quiz Score:</strong> 100.0%</p></div></div></div></div><div class="stElementContainer element-container st-emotion-cache-1vo6xi6 e1a2occc1" data-testid="stElementContainer" data-stale="false" width="100%" height="auto" overflow="visible"><div class="stAlert" data-testid="stAlert"><div data-testid="stAlertContainer" class="stAlertContainer st-emotion-cache-sbgf9b ep9twe70" role="status"><div data-testid="stAlertContentInfo" class="st-emotion-cache-1n65kaj ep9twe71"><div class="st-emotion-cache-myfgde ee8gipb0"><div class="st-emotion-cache-bzjstw e12uj6qz0" data-testid="stMarkdownContainer"><p>Excellent! You scored 100.0% in oops. You can move to the next topic or try advanced-level questions.</p></div></div></div></div></div></div><div class="stElementContainer element-container st-emotion-cache-1vo6xi6 e1a2occc1" data-testid="stElementContainer" data-stale="false" width="100%" height="auto" overflow="visible"><div class="stAlert" data-testid="stAlert"><div data-testid="stAlertContainer" class="stAlertContainer st-emotion-cache-3a140l ep9twe70" role="status"><div data-testid="stAlertContentSuccess" class="st-emotion-cache-1n65kaj ep9twe71"><div class="st-emotion-cache-myfgde ee8gipb0"><div class="st-emotion-cache-bzjstw e12uj6qz0" data-testid="stMarkdownContainer"><p>Next Action: Move Forward</p></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div data-testid="stAppIframeResizerAnchor" data-iframe-height="true" class="st-emotion-cache-1dumvfu e87roqt10"></div></main></div></div></div></div></div>
  

<div data-react-aria-top-layer="true" data-st-overlay-root="true" data-testid="portal" id="portal" class="st-emotion-cache-boztmw egah9gy0"></div><div data-react-aria-top-layer="true" data-st-overlay-root="true" id="stFloatingOverlayPortal"></div></body></html>